1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,074
Someone on your team writes one short
line in chat: "The merge is blocked."

2
00:00:09,114 --> 00:00:12,950
A merge combines two lines of
work into one. Your CTO asks why.

3
00:00:13,290 --> 00:00:17,937
That one sentence has three different
root causes, in three different layers.

4
00:00:17,977 --> 00:00:21,407
Git stops a merge when both
sides changed the same lines.

5
00:00:21,447 --> 00:00:26,338
GitHub stops a merge when a ruleset, a
named list of rules the platform enforces,

6
00:00:26,378 --> 00:00:31,208
requires a review that hasn't been given.
And GitHub Actions, the part of GitHub

7
00:00:31,248 --> 00:00:35,896
that runs automated jobs, stops a merge
when a required check hasn't reported

8
00:00:35,936 --> 00:00:40,888
success. The three symptoms look alike in
a chat message, and the three fixes have

9
00:00:40,928 --> 00:00:41,984
nothing in common.

10
00:00:44,324 --> 00:00:48,940
If you say "GitHub" when you mean Git, or
"Git" when you mean GitHub, you'll look

11
00:00:48,980 --> 00:00:53,309
for the cause in the wrong layer. This
video draws the line between the two.

12
00:00:53,349 --> 00:00:56,011
Keep that blocked merge
in mind. It comes back.

13
00:00:57,851 --> 00:01:01,516
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:01:01,556 --> 00:01:06,374
In the last video you set up the sandbox.
Today you get the two ideas that frame

15
00:01:06,414 --> 00:01:07,346
everything else.

16
00:01:07,686 --> 00:01:12,150
The first is the problem that version
control solves, stated without naming any

17
00:01:12,190 --> 00:01:16,768
product. The second is the difference
between Git, a program on your machine, and

18
00:01:16,808 --> 00:01:21,386
GitHub, a platform that hosts Git repositories.
A repository is the place where a

19
00:01:21,426 --> 00:01:26,062
project's recorded history is kept. On the
way you'll see what "distributed" means

20
00:01:26,102 --> 00:01:30,851
for your daily work, the design goals that
Git was written to meet, and a fact about

21
00:01:30,891 --> 00:01:35,241
your own Mac: it has two Git installations,
and you need to know which one is

22
00:01:35,281 --> 00:01:35,811
answering.

23
00:01:37,651 --> 00:01:39,229
Here's what you'll walk away with.

24
00:01:39,569 --> 00:01:40,982
After this video you can:

25
00:01:41,322 --> 00:01:45,438
Describe the problem that version
control solves without naming a product.

26
00:01:45,778 --> 00:01:49,946
Contrast a centralized with a distributed
system by what each clone holds.

27
00:01:50,286 --> 00:01:52,714
Name Git's design goals as
the textbook lists them.

28
00:01:53,054 --> 00:01:57,046
Say for a given feature whether it
belongs to Git the tool or to GitHub the

29
00:01:57,086 --> 00:01:57,530
platform.

30
00:01:57,870 --> 00:02:01,909
State which of the two Gits on the Mac
a command runs, and how to find out.

31
00:02:03,749 --> 00:02:08,424
The problem version control solves. In
one sentence: a version control system

32
00:02:08,464 --> 00:02:13,200
records successive states of a set of
files, so that you can recall any state,

33
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:16,997
compare any two, and see who
recorded each one, when, and why.

34
00:02:17,337 --> 00:02:20,980
Four capabilities define the category,
and each answers a question.

35
00:02:21,320 --> 00:02:25,257
Recall: what did the project look
like when release 2.3 was built?

36
00:02:25,297 --> 00:02:29,777
Compare: what differs between what runs
in production and what I have here?

37
00:02:29,817 --> 00:02:33,754
Attribute: who changed this line,
when, and with what explanation?

38
00:02:33,794 --> 00:02:37,309
Integrate: two people changed
the project at the same time.

39
00:02:37,349 --> 00:02:41,166
What is the combined result, and
where do their changes collide?

40
00:02:41,506 --> 00:02:46,221
The first three are bookkeeping. The fourth
is the reason the tool has a learning

41
00:02:46,261 --> 00:02:51,152
curve. Combining states that were produced
independently needs a model of history in

42
00:02:51,192 --> 00:02:55,906
which "independently" has a precise meaning.
Part 1 of this course gives you that

43
00:02:55,946 --> 00:02:59,428
model. So if Git has ever felt
hard, that fourth row is why.

44
00:02:59,768 --> 00:03:04,954
Centralized versus distributed. In one
sentence: in a centralized system the history

45
00:03:04,994 --> 00:03:09,246
lives on one server and each developer
holds one checked-out version.

46
00:03:09,286 --> 00:03:14,037
In a distributed system every developer's
copy contains the history, and "the

47
00:03:14,077 --> 00:03:18,765
server" is one more copy that the team
agrees to treat as the meeting point.

48
00:03:18,805 --> 00:03:20,631
Such a copy is called a clone.

49
00:03:20,971 --> 00:03:25,034
Compare the two by four questions.
Where does the history live?

50
00:03:25,074 --> 00:03:28,681
Centralized: on one server.
Distributed: in every clone.

51
00:03:28,721 --> 00:03:33,760
How is a change recorded? Centralized:
a network operation against the server.

52
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:37,798
Distributed: a local write, and
publishing is a separate step.

53
00:03:37,838 --> 00:03:42,747
What if the server is unreachable?
Centralized: nobody can record changes or

54
00:03:42,787 --> 00:03:47,370
collaborate. Distributed: local work
continues, and the exchange waits.

55
00:03:47,410 --> 00:03:51,213
What if the server's disk is
lost and there are no backups?

56
00:03:51,253 --> 00:03:56,553
Centralized: the history is gone. Distributed:
every clone still holds what it had

57
00:03:56,593 --> 00:03:57,074
fetched.

58
00:03:57,414 --> 00:04:01,715
Three consequences shape your daily work
with Git. One: commit and publish are

59
00:04:01,755 --> 00:04:06,279
different operations. A commit is one saved
snapshot of your whole project, with a

60
00:04:06,319 --> 00:04:10,842
note about who saved it and why. git commit
writes to the repository on your disk.

61
00:04:10,882 --> 00:04:15,406
git push copies commits to another repository.
"It is committed" and "it is on the

62
00:04:15,446 --> 00:04:18,912
server" are independent facts,
checked with different commands.

63
00:04:19,252 --> 00:04:23,324
Quick quiz, two options. A teammate
says the fix is committed.

64
00:04:23,364 --> 00:04:28,828
Option one: then it's on the server. Option
two: it may exist only on their laptop.

65
00:04:28,868 --> 00:04:29,889
Say it out loud.

66
00:04:34,729 --> 00:04:39,667
Option two. The commit is on their own
disk, and only a push copies it anywhere

67
00:04:39,707 --> 00:04:43,322
else. Option one is the usual
first guess, and a fair one.

68
00:04:43,662 --> 00:04:48,597
Two: your view of the server is a cached
copy. Asha pushes a commit: the server has

69
00:04:48,637 --> 00:04:53,633
it, and your clone hasn't heard. A name such
as origin/main records, in the words of

70
00:04:53,673 --> 00:04:58,608
Git's data model manual, "the last-known
state of a branch in a remote repository",

71
00:04:58,648 --> 00:05:00,586
as of your last exchange with it.

72
00:05:00,926 --> 00:05:04,083
Three: nothing in Git makes
one repository the authority.

73
00:05:04,123 --> 00:05:08,401
The team does, by convention and through
permissions on the hosting platform.

74
00:05:08,741 --> 00:05:13,892
So where does a repository live? The whole
repository, meaning the history and every

75
00:05:13,932 --> 00:05:17,847
name in it, is the dot git directory
at the top of your project.

76
00:05:17,887 --> 00:05:21,556
A clone is a copy of another
repository's objects and names.

77
00:05:21,596 --> 00:05:26,623
And the copy on a server is normally a bare
repository: the same contents, with no

78
00:05:26,663 --> 00:05:28,477
checked-out files beside them.

79
00:05:28,817 --> 00:05:33,974
Git's design goals. Git was written in 2005
for the Linux kernel, after the kernel

80
00:05:34,014 --> 00:05:37,522
project lost free use of the
proprietary tool BitKeeper.

81
00:05:37,562 --> 00:05:42,718
The book Pro Git lists the goals of the
new system, and each one explains a design

82
00:05:42,758 --> 00:05:44,429
decision you'll meet later.

83
00:05:44,769 --> 00:05:48,078
Speed: almost every
operation reads local files.

84
00:05:48,418 --> 00:05:53,518
Simple design: four object types named by
a hash of their content, plus names that

85
00:05:53,558 --> 00:05:57,718
point at them. A hash is a short
fingerprint computed from content.

86
00:05:58,058 --> 00:06:02,805
Strong support for non-linear development,
with thousands of parallel branches: a

87
00:06:02,845 --> 00:06:07,414
branch is one small name that points at
a commit, so creating one costs almost

88
00:06:07,454 --> 00:06:07,886
nothing.

89
00:06:08,226 --> 00:06:13,304
Fully distributed: every clone has the
history, and fetch and push exchange objects

90
00:06:13,344 --> 00:06:14,353
and update names.

91
00:06:14,693 --> 00:06:19,393
Able to handle large projects like the
Linux kernel efficiently: compressed pack

92
00:06:19,433 --> 00:06:22,474
files, and later, partial
clone and sparse checkout.

93
00:06:22,814 --> 00:06:27,616
Git's manual describes it as "a fast,
scalable, distributed revision control system

94
00:06:27,656 --> 00:06:32,516
with an unusually rich command set that
provides both high-level operations and full

95
00:06:32,556 --> 00:06:37,300
access to internals". The high-level
commands, such as git add, git commit and git

96
00:06:37,340 --> 00:06:41,967
merge, are called porcelain. The low-level
ones, such as git hash-object and git

97
00:06:42,007 --> 00:06:46,751
update-ref, are called plumbing. This course
uses plumbing to prove what porcelain

98
00:06:46,791 --> 00:06:46,985
did.

99
00:06:47,325 --> 00:06:51,121
Two things are absent from the
goals, and both surprise people.

100
00:06:51,161 --> 00:06:55,871
Git has no user accounts and no permissions:
the layer that hosts a repository

101
00:06:55,911 --> 00:07:00,986
decides who may read or write it. And Git
stores content, not intentions: it doesn't

102
00:07:01,026 --> 00:07:05,310
record that a file was renamed, and
it doesn't track empty directories.

103
00:07:05,650 --> 00:07:10,787
Git the tool versus GitHub the platform.
In one sentence: Git is a program on your

104
00:07:10,827 --> 00:07:15,837
machine that stores history in a .git
directory. GitHub is a hosted service that

105
00:07:15,877 --> 00:07:20,509
stores Git repositories and surrounds
them with its own objects: accounts,

106
00:07:20,549 --> 00:07:24,297
permissions, pull requests,
reviews, rulesets and workflows.

107
00:07:24,637 --> 00:07:28,117
Try it now, on paper. Draw
two columns, Git and GitHub.

108
00:07:28,157 --> 00:07:33,236
Place three things: a branch, a pull
request, and the "Verified" badge next to a

109
00:07:33,276 --> 00:07:38,483
commit. Thirty seconds. Keep the paper,
and check your answers as we fill the real

110
00:07:38,523 --> 00:07:38,867
table.

111
00:07:43,707 --> 00:07:48,525
Fill the two columns with me, and for each
row ask: does it arrive with git clone?

112
00:07:48,565 --> 00:07:53,205
Commits, directory trees, file contents
and annotated tags: Git, objects in the

113
00:07:53,245 --> 00:07:58,064
repository, and yes, they arrive. Branches
and tags: Git, names in the repository,

114
00:07:58,104 --> 00:08:02,389
and yes. The server's branches arrive
as remote-tracking branches such as

115
00:08:02,429 --> 00:08:05,588
origin/main. So your branch
belongs in the Git column.

116
00:08:05,928 --> 00:08:10,456
Author and committer name and email, and
a signature: Git, inside each commit.

117
00:08:10,496 --> 00:08:15,201
But the "Verified" badge next to a commit:
GitHub. It's GitHub's judgement of the

118
00:08:15,241 --> 00:08:19,594
signature, stored in GitHub's database,
and it doesn't arrive with a clone.

119
00:08:19,634 --> 00:08:22,815
If you put it under Git,
that's the usual first answer.

120
00:08:23,155 --> 00:08:28,852
HEAD, the index, reflogs, stashes, hooks
and the config file in the dot git folder:

121
00:08:28,892 --> 00:08:34,520
Git, but local only. Each clone has its
own. You'll meet every one of them in Part

122
00:08:34,560 --> 00:08:34,658
1.

123
00:08:34,998 --> 00:08:40,116
A pull request, a review, a comment: GitHub's
database, plus read-only refs, which

124
00:08:40,156 --> 00:08:44,897
are names, that GitHub creates on the
server for the pull request's commits.

125
00:08:44,937 --> 00:08:50,117
Issues, discussions, stars: GitHub. A fork:
GitHub, a server-side repository linked

126
00:08:50,157 --> 00:08:55,275
to its parent. To Git it's one more remote.
Rulesets and branch protection: GitHub

127
00:08:55,315 --> 00:09:00,307
settings. A release: a GitHub object that
points at a Git tag and adds notes and

128
00:09:00,347 --> 00:09:03,452
files, so the tag arrives
and the release doesn't.

129
00:09:03,792 --> 00:09:08,867
A workflow file, in the workflows folder
under dot github: Git, because it's a

130
00:09:08,907 --> 00:09:14,178
tracked file in commits. A workflow run,
its logs, its artifacts and its secrets:

131
00:09:14,218 --> 00:09:18,833
GitHub Actions. Access tokens, SSH
keys and collaborator roles: GitHub.

132
00:09:19,173 --> 00:09:24,198
The GitHub rows are described from the
course's research report, which links the

133
00:09:24,238 --> 00:09:29,515
documentation page for each. Other hosts,
such as GitLab, Bitbucket and Forgejo, run

134
00:09:29,555 --> 00:09:33,377
the same Git and surround it
with different platform objects.

135
00:09:33,717 --> 00:09:38,276
One row deserves its own sentence. A
pull request is not a Git feature.

136
00:09:38,316 --> 00:09:40,672
Git has commits, branches and merges.

137
00:09:41,012 --> 00:09:45,021
GitHub adds the pull request as a
place to discuss a proposed merge.

138
00:09:45,361 --> 00:09:49,107
And GitHub performs the merge on its
servers when you press the button.

139
00:09:50,947 --> 00:09:55,467
Three pictures now. The textbook gives
one analogy for each idea, and each has a

140
00:09:55,507 --> 00:09:58,318
breaking point that teaches
more than the analogy.

141
00:09:58,658 --> 00:10:03,656
For version control: a bound laboratory
notebook. Pages are numbered, dated and

142
00:10:03,696 --> 00:10:07,483
signed, and a correction is a
new page, never an erased one.

143
00:10:07,523 --> 00:10:12,521
It breaks in two places. A notebook is
one sequence kept by one person, while a

144
00:10:12,561 --> 00:10:17,751
repository holds many lines of work that
people extend in parallel and later join.

145
00:10:17,791 --> 00:10:22,470
And each recorded state in Git is the
whole project, not that day's notes.

146
00:10:22,810 --> 00:10:27,451
For centralized versus distributed: a
centralized system is a reference library.

147
00:10:27,491 --> 00:10:31,957
The books stay in the building, and when
the building is closed nobody reads.

148
00:10:31,997 --> 00:10:35,936
A distributed system gives every
reader a copy of the whole library.

149
00:10:36,276 --> 00:10:41,582
It breaks because library copies never need
to agree again, while repositories must.

150
00:10:41,622 --> 00:10:46,929
Each copy accumulates its own additions.
Exchanging them is an explicit act, a fetch

151
00:10:46,969 --> 00:10:50,366
or a push, and it can find
two copies in disagreement.

152
00:10:50,706 --> 00:10:55,128
For Git and GitHub: Git is a file
format with its editor, and GitHub is a

153
00:10:55,168 --> 00:11:00,017
document-management service where such
files are stored, discussed and approved.

154
00:11:00,057 --> 00:11:04,968
It breaks because GitHub also runs Git on
its servers and performs Git operations

155
00:11:05,008 --> 00:11:10,041
for you: pressing a merge button creates
commits. Platform actions change Git data,

156
00:11:10,081 --> 00:11:12,363
so you must know which layer did what.

157
00:11:14,203 --> 00:11:19,269
On the left, one server holds the only
history. Asha and Ravi each hold one version,

158
00:11:19,309 --> 00:11:23,159
and every commit is an arrow to
the server: a network operation.

159
00:11:23,199 --> 00:11:28,083
On the right, the server is a bare repository
with the history, and Asha and Ravi

160
00:11:28,123 --> 00:11:31,183
each hold the history plus
one checked-out version.

161
00:11:31,223 --> 00:11:36,289
The arrows are now push and fetch. Commit
doesn't appear as an arrow at all, because

162
00:11:36,329 --> 00:11:39,693
a commit is local. That missing
arrow is the difference.

163
00:11:40,033 --> 00:11:42,975
Now check your two columns
against the whole table.

164
00:11:43,015 --> 00:11:47,595
Commits, branches and tags arrive with a
clone. The "Verified" badge and a pull

165
00:11:47,635 --> 00:11:48,414
request don't.

166
00:11:48,754 --> 00:11:51,938
Issues, forks, rulesets and
releases live on GitHub.

167
00:11:51,978 --> 00:11:57,085
A workflow file is a tracked file, so it
arrives. Its runs, logs and secrets don't.

168
00:11:58,925 --> 00:12:03,887
Enough theory. Before we trust any transcript,
we find out which Git produced it.

169
00:12:03,927 --> 00:12:08,950
This script is marked volatile, because
its output is specific to the machine: the

170
00:12:08,990 --> 00:12:13,211
verification tool checks that it
runs and doesn't compare its output.

171
00:12:13,251 --> 00:12:15,187
Your paths may differ from mine.

172
00:12:15,527 --> 00:12:20,334
The first command is which -a git, which
lists every match in search order.

173
00:12:20,374 --> 00:12:24,664
Predict: how many lines will it
print on your Mac? Say it out loud.

174
00:12:24,704 --> 00:12:25,311
I'll wait.

175
00:12:30,151 --> 00:12:34,080
The first line is the program
that runs when you type git.

176
00:12:34,120 --> 00:12:38,871
On this machine it's the Homebrew
build, and it prints version 2.55.0.

177
00:12:38,911 --> 00:12:42,362
Homebrew is a tool that
installs software on a Mac.

178
00:12:42,402 --> 00:12:47,837
/usr/bin/git is the Git that Apple ships
with its developer tools, and it prints

179
00:12:47,877 --> 00:12:53,312
2.50.1. The third line repeats the first,
because the Homebrew directory appears

180
00:12:53,352 --> 00:12:57,898
twice in this shell's PATH, the
list of folders the shell searches.

181
00:12:57,938 --> 00:12:58,993
That's harmless.

182
00:12:59,333 --> 00:13:04,911
The difference matters. Git 2.50.1 has no
git history and no git last-modified, and

183
00:13:04,951 --> 00:13:10,596
its manual still labels git switch and git
restore as experimental, a label that Git

184
00:13:10,636 --> 00:13:16,010
removed in 2.51. Each installation also
reads its own system-level configuration

185
00:13:16,050 --> 00:13:21,560
file. You meet the Apple Git in three
situations. A script that calls /usr/bin/git

186
00:13:21,600 --> 00:13:27,178
by its full path. A scheduled job or graphical
tool that starts with a shorter PATH

187
00:13:27,218 --> 00:13:31,171
than your terminal. And a
colleague's Mac without Homebrew.

188
00:13:31,511 --> 00:13:34,009
Now ask the Git that
answered how it was built.

189
00:13:34,349 --> 00:13:39,596
Three lines are worth reading now.
default-hash: sha1 means new repositories name

190
00:13:39,636 --> 00:13:45,014
objects with SHA-1. SHA-1: SHA1_DC means
the implementation detects known collision

191
00:13:45,054 --> 00:13:50,171
attacks. And default-ref-format: files
means branch and tag names are stored as

192
00:13:50,211 --> 00:13:54,153
plain files, which is why this
course can show them with cat.

193
00:13:54,193 --> 00:13:56,372
Chapter 3 covers the alternatives.

194
00:13:56,712 --> 00:14:03,288
A version note from the textbook: Git
2.55.0, released on the twenty-ninth of June

195
00:14:03,328 --> 00:14:09,822
2026, is what Homebrew installed here. The
latest release is Git 2.56.0, from the

196
00:14:09,862 --> 00:14:16,518
twenty-eighth of September 2026, which adds
a few commands and no security fix that

197
00:14:16,558 --> 00:14:23,294
2.55.0 lacks. Upgrading is optional, and
the course marks what needs it as "added in

198
00:14:23,334 --> 00:14:25,231
Git 2.56, not run here".

199
00:14:25,571 --> 00:14:32,128
And one note on the other layer. The GitHub
CLI, gh, is a separate program: a way to

200
00:14:32,168 --> 00:14:36,998
work with GitHub by typing
commands. Installed here is 2.88.1.

201
00:14:37,038 --> 00:14:43,438
The latest on the first of October 2026 is
2.102.0, with six security-fix releases

202
00:14:43,478 --> 00:14:47,601
in between, so upgrade it
before the GitHub chapters.

203
00:14:47,641 --> 00:14:53,649
From 2.91.0 the CLI collects pseudonymous
usage telemetry unless you opt out.

204
00:14:53,689 --> 00:14:59,854
The textbook gives the two ways to opt
out, and says that neither was run here,

205
00:14:59,894 --> 00:15:03,310
because the setting
doesn't exist in 2.88.1.

206
00:15:05,150 --> 00:15:06,798
Five mistakes to watch for.

207
00:15:07,138 --> 00:15:10,066
Saying "it is committed" to
mean "it is on the server".

208
00:15:10,106 --> 00:15:14,491
Root cause: commit and publish are different
operations; git commit writes to your

209
00:15:14,531 --> 00:15:17,459
disk and git push copies
commits to another repository.

210
00:15:17,799 --> 00:15:21,033
Treating origin/main as the
live state of the server.

211
00:15:21,073 --> 00:15:25,915
Root cause: it is a cached copy, the
last-known state as of your last exchange.

212
00:15:26,255 --> 00:15:29,482
Assuming a clone is a backup
that somebody made for you.

213
00:15:29,522 --> 00:15:34,207
Root cause: distributed means that copies
are possible, not that they are made; a

214
00:15:34,247 --> 00:15:38,932
commit that exists only on one laptop is
one disk failure away from not existing.

215
00:15:39,272 --> 00:15:42,897
Looking for a pull request, a
review or a ruleset inside Git.

216
00:15:42,937 --> 00:15:47,764
Root cause: those are GitHub objects in
GitHub's database, and they do not arrive

217
00:15:47,804 --> 00:15:48,545
with a clone.

218
00:15:48,885 --> 00:15:53,947
A command from the course is "not a git
command". Root cause: the shell found the

219
00:15:53,987 --> 00:15:59,111
Apple Git first; which -a git and git
--version show it, and the fix is to put the

220
00:15:59,151 --> 00:16:01,190
Homebrew directory first in PATH.

221
00:16:03,030 --> 00:16:07,941
Now, out of the lab. The evaluation score
of an LLM application drops from one week

222
00:16:07,981 --> 00:16:12,594
to the next. To explain the drop you need
the prompt templates, the evaluation

223
00:16:12,634 --> 00:16:17,545
configuration and the scoring code exactly
as they were when last week's number was

224
00:16:17,585 --> 00:16:22,377
produced. If they live in one repository
and the report recorded a commit ID, one

225
00:16:22,417 --> 00:16:27,149
command restores that state. If they live
on a shared drive, the question has no

226
00:16:27,189 --> 00:16:31,802
reliable answer. Datasets and model
weights need additional tools, which later

227
00:16:31,842 --> 00:16:32,697
chapters cover.

228
00:16:33,037 --> 00:16:37,386
And the second half of the picture:
GitHub is unreachable for an hour.

229
00:16:37,426 --> 00:16:42,589
You can still commit, create branches,
compare versions and search history, because

230
00:16:42,629 --> 00:16:47,416
all of that reads your own dot git folder.
You can't push, fetch, open a pull

231
00:16:47,456 --> 00:16:49,171
request or start a workflow.

232
00:16:51,011 --> 00:16:56,507
Your turn. Do Exercise 1.2,
Level 1, "Git or GitHub?", in

233
00:16:56,547 --> 00:16:59,712
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

234
00:17:00,052 --> 00:17:04,611
Before you look anything up, decide each
item from the rule you heard today: where

235
00:17:04,651 --> 00:17:07,583
does this thing live, and
would a git clone carry it?

236
00:17:07,623 --> 00:17:10,780
Write your prediction beside
each item first, then check.

237
00:17:12,620 --> 00:17:17,263
Q1: "Why can git log work without a
network connection while git push cannot?

238
00:17:17,303 --> 00:17:19,878
What does your answer
imply about backups?"

239
00:17:24,718 --> 00:17:29,350
Answer out loud. A strong answer says
where the history that git log reads is

240
00:17:29,390 --> 00:17:34,143
stored, and what git push has to reach.
It separates recording from publishing.

241
00:17:34,183 --> 00:17:39,058
And it doesn't stop at the first half. It
draws the consequence for a commit that

242
00:17:39,098 --> 00:17:43,609
exists in exactly one place, in both
directions, the laptop and the server.

243
00:17:45,449 --> 00:17:50,295
Let's land this. You should now be able
to say: version control gives me recall,

244
00:17:50,335 --> 00:17:54,753
comparison, attribution and integration,
and integration is the hard one.

245
00:17:54,793 --> 00:17:59,700
In a distributed system every clone holds
the history, so committing is local and

246
00:17:59,740 --> 00:18:04,830
publishing is a separate step. Git has no
accounts and no permissions, and it stores

247
00:18:04,870 --> 00:18:06,296
content, not intentions.

248
00:18:06,636 --> 00:18:11,833
Git is a program and a data format. GitHub
is a platform that hosts Git repositories

249
00:18:11,873 --> 00:18:15,698
and adds its own objects, most
of which a clone doesn't carry.

250
00:18:15,738 --> 00:18:20,124
And on my Mac, which -a git tells me
which of two Gits I am talking to.

251
00:18:20,464 --> 00:18:25,392
And that chat message, "The merge is blocked"?
You now have three questions for it.

252
00:18:25,432 --> 00:18:29,701
Is it Git, where the branch lives? Is
it GitHub, where the review lives?

253
00:18:29,741 --> 00:18:32,096
Or GitHub Actions, where the checks run?

254
00:18:33,936 --> 00:18:37,392
Read sections 1.2 to 1.6 of Chapter 1.

255
00:18:37,732 --> 00:18:42,376
Note the output of the three version
commands on your machine: which -a git, git

256
00:18:42,416 --> 00:18:44,659
--version, git version --build-options.

257
00:18:44,999 --> 00:18:50,340
Challenge: take five things from a repository
page of a project you use, and decide

258
00:18:50,380 --> 00:18:55,722
for each whether a git clone would carry
it. Keep the list; V115 returns to it with

259
00:18:55,762 --> 00:18:56,111
proof.

260
00:18:56,451 --> 00:18:59,856
Today you drew the line
between a tool and a platform.

261
00:18:59,896 --> 00:19:05,024
Try the two-column exercise before the
next video. Next time: a first repository,

262
00:19:05,064 --> 00:19:09,554
read file by file. Until then, look
at the state first and type second.

263
00:19:09,594 --> 00:19:11,085
See you in the next one.
